    "result_text": "The full test suite passes. \n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the daily-limit rule for creating owners: once **100 or more owners have already been registered today** (by `registrationDate`), a create request is rejected with **HTTP 429 Too Many Requests**.\n\nFollowing the project's OfficeFloor function-injection conventions (mirroring the existing `EnsureCityUnderCapacity` capacity rule), I added:\n\n1. **`Registrations.java`** \u2014 a small package-private helper (parallel to `Cities`) with `countOn(repo, date)`, the single place that defines \"owners registered on a day.\"\n\n2. **`EnsureUnderDailyLimit.java`** \u2014 a pipeline step that counts today's registrations and throws when `>= DAILY_LIMIT` (100), rejecting before `BuildOwner` persists an over-limit owner.\n\n3. **`DailyLimitExceededException.java`** \u2014 a checked exception carrying the date and limit.\n\n4. **`DailyLimitExceededExceptionHandler.java`** \u2014 the escalation handler, building a `ProblemDetail` via the shared `ProblemDetails.build(...)` and responding `429`.\n\n5. **`org.springframework.samples.petclinic.rest.escalation.DailyLimitExceededException.yml`** \u2014 maps the exception to its handler.\n\n6. **`owners.POST.yml`** \u2014 wired the new `ensureUnderDailyLimit` step into the pipeline (after `ensureCityUnderCapacity`, under `transaction` governance like its neighbors).\n\nEach class keeps a single responsibility, and the counting logic is reused via the `Registrations` helper rather than inlined. `AcceptanceTest.coreRejectsOverDailyLimit` and the full suite are green.",
